  "notes": "Built by code (model_sources/Q3050235_murallas_de_avila.py, tools/meshkit.py) with AI assistance. The plan follows the OpenStreetMap outline of the wall (inner face, outer face and every tower bump) node by node; the towers are found as the runs of outer nodes more than 5 m from the inner face and their corners rounded (84 towers, the literature counts 87). The wall follows the terrain: EU-DEM 25 m sampled every 10 m along the wall, wall walk 9.8 m above the outside ground (smoothed over 50 m), parapet 1 m, merlons with pyramidal caps (top ≈ 12.4 m above the ground), foundations 6 m below the lowest nearby ground (down to −59.5 m on the west side, above the Adaja); so the top of the wall is a smooth slope where the real wall walk climbs in steps. Towers rise 2.4 m above the walk (the shallow south towers 1.2 m), with a string course under the parapet. The nine gates are dark arched openings on both faces; the Puerta del Alcázar and the Puerta de San Vicente have two tall towers joined high up by an arch. The cimorro (the cathedral apse built into the east wall) is modelled from the cathedral's OSM outline as a two-tier crenellated drum with a machicolation on corbels; the cathedral itself, the houses and palaces built against the wall, the Espadaña del Carmen, stairs, railings and the barbicans are not. Heights of towers, gates and cimorro are from photos (±10–15 %). Footprint: a keyhole ring — the outer outline of the wall, towers and cimorro grown by 1.5 m, a slit, and the town side of the wall shrunk 1.5 m into the town — so only the wall is cleared, not the old town inside (even-odd)."
